Id\":\"1408587934724886\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"inactive\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-10-23T18:09:12.202Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-10-22T07:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-10-27T17:11:25.903Z\",\"daysRunning\":5,\"media\":{\"type\":\"image\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/image/58d72091a15bad859c01e4f3bc250733b067463c9b3a5c5d943ac30d2cb567d5.png\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"100233209082814\",\"name\":\"Aurivita\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/profile_picture/100233209082814.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"100233209082814\",\"liveAdsCount\":106,\"reach30d\":26818,\"totalReach\":230027,\"countriesCount\":1,\"facebookLikes\":99,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"Capsaicin\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"SEE DETAILS\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"http://aurivita.co/\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"aurivita.co\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Cayenne Pepper\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":\"Support your cardiovascular health with our capsaicin softgels, a premium supplement meticulously formulated for those who prioritize long-term heart vitality.\"},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":null,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":null,\"duplicates\":175,\"reachDelta1d\":null,\"reachDelta7d\":null,\"reachDelta30d\":null},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":[\"US\"],\"mainCountry\":null,\"gender\":null,\"ageMin\":null,\"ageMax\":null},\"rank\":{\"positionInPage\":26,\"currentRank\":26,\"rankDelta\":null,\"improvementPct\":63.41},\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":null,\"isLowReach\":true,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}},{\"id\":\"facebook_4372585229724728\",\"collationId\":\"1312627477000968\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"inactive\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-08-27T18:04:01.196Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-05-30T07:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-06-01T07:00:00.000Z\",\"daysRunning\":2,\"media\":{\"type\":\"video\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/thumbnails/b85849f52ef058f06b567e6e1c999e7c71c6f8d5af30592ae96c3923c85ac0fb.jpg\",\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/video/676583e1e9837fd08e4dc33f03a72ef209fd1bb6692c58a57dc49e018c498070.mp4\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"1084300221438954\",\"name\":\"ThrobbingHeat-Storyfz111\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/profile_picture/1084300221438954.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"1084300221438954\",\"liveAdsCount\":0,\"reach30d\":0,\"totalReach\":4323,\"countriesCount\":5,\"facebookLikes\":0,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"Juliet Venn's eyes snapped open and she shot up in bed. Darkness pressed in around her, thick and silent.\\n\\nMoonlight cut through the window. She scanned the room, tense and wary, until her gaze landed on something that made her eyes go wide.\\n\\nThe place was familiar, but off in a way she couldn't pin down. This was her home.\\n\\nShe grabbed her phone and stared at the screen.\\n\\nJuly 4th, 2123. 11:14 PM.\\n\\n'I came back to a month before the apocalypse?' Her mind raced. 'I was just fighting off a gang of looters, and then the earthquake hit...'\\n\\nShe turned to the window. 30 feet away, a few old men sat under a streetlight in shorts and T-shirts, playing cards.\\n\\nThey did this every summer, coming out around nine and staying until midnight before heading home. Juliet had seen that scene more times than she could count, but never once had it made her jaw drop like this.\\n\\nA flash of memory hit her. She raised her phone, switched on the camera, and zoomed in on the old men.\\n\\nShe had seen them before. Right before the apocalypse started, on a night when she stayed up late binge watching a show, she watched them tear each other apart.\\n\\nAt the time, she thought they'd gotten into a fight over cards. When they stopped moving, she called 911. The ambulance took them away and she forgot all about it. She hadn't known that was the opening act of the end of the world.\\n\\nOn her zoomed in screen, one of the card players lunged at the man closest to him. A scream tore through the night, and then they were all over each other, biting and thrashing.\\n\\nThe whole thing lasted two minutes. Then they lay still on the ground.\\n\\nJuliet kept recording. She was about to hit stop when her breath caught in her throat. The body that should have been dead, or at least taken hours to turn, slowly pushed itself up from the ground.\\n\\nIts movements were stiff and jerky as it shuffled toward home, but after a few steps, it collapsed again.\\n\\n\\\"Ding. Apocalypse Awakening System activated.\\\"\\n\\nJuliet had just lowered her phone when a stranger's voice spoke in her ear. Before she could even process what was happening, a line of text appeared in front of her eyes out of thin air.\\n\\n[Accept Beginner Quest?]\\n\\n'What is this thing?' She thought, stunned. 'I never ran into this in my previous life.'\\n\\nJuliet froze for one second, then accepted. The next moment, she was standing in a completely unfamiliar place. A thatched hut, a well, and a garden plot about ten square feet.\\n\\n'Is this one of those pocket dimensions I've heard about?' she tried to wrap her head around it.\\n\\nIn the apocalypse, strange things happened. In her previous life, she had awakened a power too, but it was invisibility, not this. So she rolled with it and accepted what she was seeing.\\n\\nJuliet was over the moon. She pulled up the beginner quest panel right away.\\n\\n[Stockpile Survival Supplies (0/1,700 dollars)]\\n\\n'So the system wants me to start hoarding?' She was thrilled. She stepped out of the pocket dimension and looked around her room, scanning for anything worth taking.\\n\\nHer eyes landed on the computer on the desk. If she remembered correctly, her brother Ryan Venn had bought it for her last week. 1,700 dollars, exactly.\\n\\nWith a single thought, Juliet and the computer were back inside the pocket dimension. The text on the quest panel turned from red to green.\\n\\n[Stockpile Survival Supplies (9,500/1,700 dollars) Completed. Reward: 50 square feet of Pocket Dimension.]\\n\\nJuliet stared at the number, 9,500 dollars, and froze. Ryan had told her he picked it up on his way home from work, just some cheap thing he grabbed on a whim. He said it cost him 1,700 dollars.\\n\\nIt hit her all at once. Her eyes stung. She took a deep breath and kept going with the rest of the beginner quests, hauling more things into the pocket dimension.\\n\\nThe ergonomic chair, 2,900 dollars. The couch, 12,000 dollars. The TV, 8,000 dollars. A trash can, 22 dollars. A pair of socks, 1 dollar.\\n\\n'One dollar?' She frowned at the socks she had just grabbed, recognized them as Ryan's, and tossed them right back out.\\n\\nJuliet slowly figured out how things worked. Basically, in the beginning, 350 dollars worth of supplies bought her about ten square feet of the pocket dimension.\\n\\nShe didn't know if the price would go up later, but as long as she kept throwing things in, her pocket dimension would keep expanding.\\n\\nShe also discovered that as long as something wasn't alive, its condition here would stay the same forever. The ice cream she had brought in an hour ago and forgotten about was still rock solid, sitting right where she left it.\\n\\n'This is the perfect hiding spot for the apocalypse. But...' The light in her eyes dimmed. She thought about her parents and her brother, living right next door, and her fists clenched tight. 'If only they could come in here too.'\\n\\nJuliet was strong enough to protect them this time, having lived through it all before. But she couldn't guarantee there wouldn't be accidents. No matter what, she wasn't letting them leave her again.\\n\\nHours of nonstop work passed. She had nearly emptied her apartment. The pocket dimension had upgraded too, the thatched hut turning into a mud house, and then into a brick one.\\n\\nJust as she was about to crawl back into bed and catch up on sleep, the system chimed in her ear again.\\n\\n[Congratulations. You have completed all Beginner Quests. Reward: 1 Family Member Slot. Please assign a family member within 24 hours.]\\n\\nJuliet stood frozen in place, her heart racing with excitement. 'Other people can come in?'\\n\\nShe stepped out of the pocket dimension and headed next door.\\n\\nShe lived in a penthouse duplex. Upstairs and downstairs together totaled 3,000 square feet, with a 1000-square-foot rooftop garden out front. One apartment per floor. Her parents and brother lived in the unit next door, on the same top floor.\\n\\nBetween the two rooftops, there was a wall about four and a half feet tall. It wasn't load-bearing, so they had knocked it out and replaced it with a small iron door that no one really used.\\n\\nThat way, Juliet could go next door for dinner every day but still have her own private space to live in.\\n\\n\\\"Juliet, are you up? I made pot pies and oatmeal, your favorites. They're still hot. Eat something before you go back to sleep, okay? Ahhh!\\\"\\n\\nOn the stairs, Monica Venn, who had come to call her daughter for breakfast, saw the empty living room and froze. Her face went pale, thinking burglars had broken in.\\n\\nShe rushed downstairs and saw Juliet pushing through the front door. Tears started rolling down her face. \\\"Are you alright? Let me get a look at you.\\\"\\n\\nShe circled Juliet twice, checked her over, and finally let out a breath of relief when she confirmed her daughter was unharmed.\\n\\nRichard Venn and Ryan heard Monica scream from next door and came running. They were just as shocked to see the stripped-down living room.\\n\\n\\\"Dad. Mom. Ryan.\\\" Juliet's voice trembled. She couldn't remember the last time she had seen any of their faces.\\n\\nIn her previous life, they all died protecting her in the apocalypse. She was the last one left, and she held on for another ten years alone until that earthquake, the one that leveled everything, sent her back here.\\n\\nShe threw herself into Monica's arms and hugged her tight, crying tears of joy.\\n\\nMonica thought she was scared from the supposed burglary and rubbed her back, trying to comfort her.\\n\\nThe two men stood there uselessly. One of them wanted to hug Juliet, the other wanted to ruffle her hair, but neither could bring themselves to move.\\n\\nChapter 2 Family Member Binding\\nAfter surviving ten years alone in the apocalypse, Juliet had forgotten what it felt like to cry.\\n\\nWithout her parents and brother to protect her, her home got taken, her food got stolen. She drifted from place to place, just trying to stay alive while escaping all the shady men out to take advantage of her.\\n\\nCornered with nowhere left to run, she was forced to kill for the first time. The second came easier. Her hands soon stopped shaking, turning cold and numb over time.\\n\\nAs the bodies piled up, fewer dared to cross her. In the end, hers became the name people whispered in fear.\\n\\nJuliet looked up at Monica, right there in front of her, and wiped her tears away. A small smile touched her lips as she spoke, trying to reassure her mother. \\\"Mom, don't worry. No one broke in. I'm fine.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Then what happened here? Juliet, don't be scared. You're sleeping at my place tonight.\\\"\\n\\nMonica turned and smacked Richard hard. \\\"This is your fault. What kind of cheap door did you buy her? Burglars just walked right in.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"My fault.\\\" Richard frowned and nodded along.\\n\\nHe had passed on buying a house because he was worried about security, and now the penthouse got robbed anyway. He decided right then to install an electric fence on the door.\\n\\n\\\"It wasn't burglars,\\\" Juliet said. \\\"I moved everything out.\\\"\\n\\nShe stopped Ryan from calling 911 and looked at the three of them, her expression serious. \\\"Dad. Mom. Ryan. Do you trust me?\\\"\\n\\nThey didn't understand why she was asking, but they looked at each other, then back at her, and nodded without hesitation.\\n\\n\\\"What if I told you the apocalypse is coming in a month? Do you still trust me?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"You had a nightmare?\\\" Ryan asked.\\n\\n\\\"No.\\\" Juliet pulled out her phone and found the video she had recorded the night before. \\\"Watch this.\\\"\\n\\nThe three of them crowded around the screen. When the video ended, each of them had a different reaction.\\n\\nRichard said, \\\"Those old guys really went at it. No wonder I heard sirens this morning.\\n\\nMonica added, \\\"They play cards there every day. Old men have bad tempers. Fights happen.\\\"\\n\\nRyan shrugged. \\\"This doesn't prove anything.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"What if I told you I have a pocket dimension?\\\"\\n\\nJuliet locked eyes with Ryan. They were five years apart. He had basically raised her.\\n\\nMonica used to be a surgeon, working double shifts at the hospital, coming home late most nights.\\n\\nRichard ran a bunch of different businesses, first a fruit stand, then a restaurant, then a tutoring center. The money kept coming in, but he kept getting busier.\\n\\nLater, when things got better, Monica quit the hospital to stay home with the kids. That was when life finally settled down.\\n\\nBefore that, Juliet followed Ryan everywhere. He washed her clothes, fed her meals, showed her how to beat video game levels, and even dragged her along when he skipped class. They never had that awkward gap older and younger siblings sometimes get.\\n\\n\\\"Mom, just so you know,\\\" Juliet said, \\\"I'm going to take you somewhere. You'll find yourself in a different place all of a sudden. Dad and Ryan will disappear too. But don't be scared.\\\"\\n\\nMonica gently took hold of Juliet's arm. \\\"You don't sound right.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"I'm fine.\\\" Juliet took her hand, her face dead serious. \\\"Are you ready?\\\"\\n\\nMonica hesitated, then nodded. The next second, both of them vanished.\\n\\nRyan sucked in a breath. Richard stumbled back two steps, his face pale.\\n\\n\\\"Ryan... where's your mother and sister?\\\" Richard asked.\\n\\nInside the pocket dimension, Monica flinched at the sudden change of scenery.\\n\\nDing. Family member bound.\\n\\nThe system voice spoke in Juliet's ear. A panel appeared in front of her, displaying all of Monica's information in crisp text. Monica couldn't see any of it.\\n\\nName: Monica Venn\\n\\nGender: Female\\n\\nAge: 47\\n\\nHeight: 5'4\\\"\\n\\nBlood Type: B\\n\\nProfession: Former Surgeon\\n\\nRelationship: Mother\\n\\nLoyalty: Never Betray\\n\\nJuliet scanned the panel and hit the bind button. Another message popped up.\\n\\n[Family member can only enter and exit with Owner. Owner may unbind any member at any time. Upon leaving the system, all memories of the pocket dimension will be erased.]\\n\\n'This is basically a cheat code.' Juliet laughed softly and closed the panel.\\n\\n\\\"Mom, let's go back. If we stay too long, Dad and Ryan will start panicking.\\\"\\n\\nMonica had just finished walking through the small brick house and spotted all the missing items from their home. She looked a little dazed as she nodded, took Juliet's hand, and let herself be pulled back out.\\n\\n\\\"Do you guys believe me now?\\\" Juliet helped Monica sit on the edge of the bed and turned to look at Ryan and Richard.\\n\\nTheir faces had gone dark. After a long silence, Ryan asked in a low voice, \\\"How long until the apocalypse?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"One month. But it could happen sooner. Hard to say. Ryan, do you remember those zombie movies you used to show me?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Yeah. Is the apocalypse like that?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"No. Worse.\\\"\\n\\nThe room fell silent. No one spoke for a few minutes. Then Ryan pulled out his phone and called his office to request time off.\\n\\nHe had just finished a big project that took over a year. His vacation days had stacked up to nearly three months.\\n\\nHe was right in the gap between projects, and since he had been the lead on the last one, the company approved his time off right away. They told him to rest up and recharge before coming back to work.\\n\\nRyan hung up and looked at Juliet. \\\"Alright. What do we need to do to get ready?\\\"\\n\\nRichard and Monica sat up straight, waiting for her to speak.\\n\\nJuliet looked at the three of them and felt warmth spread through her chest. She was moved by how they always spoiled her like this, trusting her without question no matter what.\\n\\n\\\"The first three years are going to be brutal,\\\" she said. \\\"Although it still has four distinct seasons, summer temperatures can reach up to around 150°F, while winter can drop to about -85°F.\\n\\n\\\"No power. No running water. Zombies everywhere. We need to stockpile food, clothes, and medicine first.\\\"\\n\\nRyan nodded thoughtfully. \\\"But for the first three years, the zombies aren't our biggest problem, are they?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Right.\\\" Juliet nodded back. \\\"People are.\\\"\\n\\nWhen food ran out, human nature showed its teeth.\\n\\nIn Juliet's previous life, the neighbors climbed up the fire escape to the top floor, jumped from the roof down to the garden, broke into their home, and stole their food before throwing the whole family out.\\n\\nJuliet told them what happened back then. The three of them listened, and their faces gradually went cold.\\n\\n\\\"Ryan, let's go. We're heading to the home improvement store.\\\" Richard slammed his hand on the table and stood up.\\n\\nHe had started from nothing, selling fruit on the streets to climb his way up. Having a daughter had softened him over the years, but he would never let anyone push his family around.\\n\\nThis time, anyone who came for them would get cut down.\\n\\nChapter 3 The Whole Family Hits the Stores\\n\\\"Dad, I'll take care of the rooftop,\\\" Ryan suggested. \\\"You and Mom hit the market. Juliet and I can't tell good produce from bad. You two have to handle the fresh stuff. Let's split up, so we move faster.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Deal. That's the plan. Honey, let's go. Get changed and we're out the door.\\\" Richard pulled Monica to her feet.\\n\\nAs they walked away, Juliet could still hear him muttering under his breath. \\\"They want to mess with my daughter? I'll bury them.\\\"\\n\\nRyan waited until his parents were out of sight before turning back to Juliet. They locked eyes, and he asked the question that had been eating at him. \\\"How long did the apocalypse last?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"I don't know. But from what I saw, it wouldn't end within 20 years.\\\"\\n\\nAt least not before she died, and that was the 15th year.\\n\\nRyan's throat bobbed. \\\"And you? How many years did you go through alone?\\\"\\n\\nJuliet hadn't expected that question.\\n\\nRyan was quick on the uptake. He had put it together from the way Juliet looked at them when she first woke up, plus that pocket dimension of hers.\\n\\nShe hadn't seen them in a long time. And her eyes, even though she tried to soften them around the family, still carried that sharp edge. The kind one only got from surviving life and death.\\n\\nHis little sister had never gone without, even back when the family was crammed into a bungalow with barely enough room to breathe.\\n\\nRichard and Monica didn't expect much from her. As long as she stayed out of trouble and stayed happy and healthy, that was enough.\\n\\nRyan felt the same way. They could make the money. Juliet didn't need to struggle. As long as she stayed away from drugs, they'd make sure she was set for life. But in the end, they left her alone anyway.\\n\\nRyan sighed, closed the distance, and reached out to ruffle Juliet's hair. \\\"This time, I'm with you to the end.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Yeah.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Go eat. Fuel up. Then we shop.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Okay.\\\"\\n\\nJuliet hadn't tasted anything this good in years. The hunger from her previous life came rushing back. She tore through more than a dozen meat pies, and Ryan had to stop her before she made herself sick.\\n\\nWhen they finished eating, they went downstairs and split up. Ryan headed to the home improvement store to handle the rooftop, while Juliet went to buy everything else.\\n\\nShe drove to the largest warehouse club in the city, ready for a full-scale looting spree. Juliet no longer worried about moral boundaries. The apocalypse was coming, and everyone's only rule was to survive.\\n\\nShe had thought about going overseas for this, but she was afraid the apocalypse might start early.\\n\\nThose old men who had dropped dead playing cards downstairs were clearly infected, which meant the end could begin at any moment. She didn't dare separate from her family.\\n\\nThe moment she stepped through the supermarket doors, the system voice spoke in her head again.\\n\\n\\\"Surveillance blocker activated. User will no longer appear on camera.\\\"\\n\\nJuliet smiled to herself. 'This system is surprisingly thoughtful.'\\n\\nShe needed too many things, and buying them the normal way would have taken countless trips back and forth. On top of that, she didn't want anyone noticing and causing unnecessary trouble.\\n\\nWith the help from the system, the most efficient way was a silent looting spree.\\n\\nShe pushed a cart up to the second floor. Rows of steel shelving stood packed with every product imaginable. On the main aisle, employees handed out samples to customers.\\n\\nThe shelves were a good two and a half feet deep. Not many staff members were around, and the store needed enough inventory to meet demand. Even if someone reached all the way to the back, the security cameras wouldn't see a thing.\\n\\nJuliet made a beeline for the grain section and started loading. Fifty 40-pound boxes of rice went straight into her pocket dimension.\\n\\nThen came the flour, all-purpose, bread, and cake varieties packed in 30-pound boxes. One full shelf got her 50 boxes, gone in seconds.\\n\\nShe grabbed 20 boxes of black beans, brown rice, peanuts, and quinoa, each box weighing four pounds, and 30 jugs of vegetable, corn, olive, sunflower, and avocado oil, each jug holding one point three gallons.\\n\\nIt was a Tuesday at 9 AM, way too early for most shoppers, so no one noticed anything weird.\\n\\nShe moved to the condiments aisle and pulled 20 bottles each of soy sauce, apple cider vinegar, white vinegar, ketchup, and yellow mustard.\\n\\nThen she grabbed 50 bags each of cinnamon, dried chili peppers, star anise, bay leaves, fennel, cloves, oregano, and paprika.\\n\\nFor salt, both iodized and non-iodized, she took 70 boxes with ten bags per box.\\n\\nThe apocalypse wasn't here yet, so she couldn't clean out the entire store. People still needed to buy groceries.\\n\\nAnd she didn't know how much her pocket dimension could hold, so she needed to grab as many different categories as possible. If she filled it up with just one item, there wouldn't be any room left for the rest.\\n\\nRight as that thought crossed her mind, the system chimed again.\\n\\n\\\"Ding. Limited-time double reward quest triggered. For every 350 dollars of supplies stocked within one hour, pocket dimension expands by 20 square feet.\\\"\\n\\nJuliet's lips curled up as she picked up the pace. She stopped in front of the refrigerated section packed with high-quality meat and started loading her cart right in front of the employee.\\n\\nPork ribs, three pounds per tray, 40 dollars each. Ten trays, 400 dollars.\\n\\nSausage links, three pounds per tray, 30 dollars each. Ten trays, 300 dollars.\\n\\nPork tenderloin, three pounds per tray, 25 dollars each. Ten trays, 250 dollars.\\n\\nPork belly, three pounds per tray, 30 dollars each. Ten trays, 300 dollars.\\n\\nBlack pork ribs, two pounds per tray, 40 dollars each. Ten trays, 400 dollars.\\n\\nBacon, three pounds per tray, 40 dollars each. Ten trays, 400 dollars.\\n\\nBlack pork bone-in chops, one and a half pounds per tray, 40 dollars each. Ten trays, 400 dollars.\\n\\nGround beef, two pounds per tray, 40 dollars each. Ten trays, 400 dollars.\\n\\nSteaks, one and a half pounds per tray, 60 dollars each. Ten trays, 600 dollars.\\n\\nGround lamb, one pound per pack, 35 dollars each. Ten packs, 350 dollars.\\n\\nTotal for the meat came to 3,800 dollars, and she would have grabbed more if the refrigerated section hadn't been cleaned out.\\n\\nThe employee figured she was a delivery shopper. There was no limit sign posted, so he couldn't say much. He just trudged off to restock.\\n\\nJuliet pushed her cart to a blind spot in the aisle. With the system covering the cameras, she made sure no one was around and dumped everything into her pocket dimension before heading for the electronics section.\\n\\nThe apocalypse meant no power and no water, but her pocket dimension had both, so appliances would still wear out.\\n\\nAnd with prep time on her side this time around, she could grab home generators and solar panels too. Small appliances were definitely on the list.\\n\\nThe three robot vacuums Juliet took were 1,200 dollars each, totaling 3,600 dollars.\\n\\nShe grabbed every air purifier she could see, ten of them at 1,300 dollars each, totaling 13,000 dollars.\\n\\nFor dishwashers, she went for the most expensive ones at 8,500 dollars each and took two, totaling 17,000 dollars.\\n\\nFive bladeless fan and heater combos at 1,300 dollars each made another 6,500 dollars.\\n\\nShe decided to forget about air conditioners since the family already had those at home, and she could just take them apart and stash them in her pocket dimension.\\n\\nTwo PS5 consoles for Ryan came next at 1,300 dollars each, totaling 2,600 dollars, followed by the entire drone shelf, six of them at 1,700 dollars each, adding 10,200 dollars.\\n\\nBy the time she left the electronics section, 52,900 dollars worth of goods had been collected.\\n\\nChapter 4 Aiming for the C-Suite\\nJuliet hurried away from the electronics section and spotted a display of food storage container sets. Her eyes lit up.\\n\\nThe pocket dimension could keep food in its freshest possible state. If she cooked meals in her downtime and packed them into food storage container stacked on shelves, she could just grab one whenever she got hungry. Fast and easy.\\n\\nEach box held 12 containers and cost 67 dollars. Juliet moved ten boxes into the pocket dimension first, totaling 670 dollars.\\n\\nKnife sets came in packs of seven at 230 dollars per set. She took ten sets, 2,300 dollars in total.\\n\\nToilet paper came in 32-roll packs at 90 dollars each. Ten packs, 900 dollars total.\\n\\nFlushable wipes came in eight-packs at 30 dollars each. This stuff was a game changer, and anyone who had used it knew exactly why. Juliet grabbed 20 packs first, 600 dollars total.\\n\\nTrash bags came in boxes of 360 at 23 dollars per box. She grabbed 100 boxes, totaling 2,300 dollars.\\n\\nTotal for the run was 6,770 dollars.\\n\\nIn less than 30 minutes, Juliet had looted 63,470 dollars worth of supplies. Based on the system's exchange rate, her pocket dimension should have expanded by 3,626 square feet by now.\\n\\nJuliet couldn't contain her excitement. She found a hidden corner with no one around and slipped inside the pocket dimension.\\n\\nWhen she saw all the supplies she had just looted piled up in the open space, and the small brick house she had upgraded through the night now turned into a two-story villa, she felt on top of the world.\\n\\nShe decided to keep hoarding.\\n\\nShe was going to shoot for C-suite real estate, the kind of super mansion where one woke up on a bed so massive it could fit half the city, and needed a car just to get to the bathroom.\\n\\nJuliet stepped out of the pocket dimension, feeling great, and headed downstairs to the beauty section first.\\n\\nThe apocalypse sun was brutal. Everyone turned dark as hell. Black was beautiful, sure, but she didn't want it, and neither did all those rich socialites.\\n\\nSo sunscreen would be great for trading later. High demand guaranteed. She cleared the entire shelf, 70 tubes at 67 dollars each, totaling 4,690 dollars.\\n\\nShe grabbed 50 tubes of high-capacity hand cream at 60 dollars each, totaling 3,000 dollars.\\n\\nThen she took 20 boxes of toothbrushes, six brushes per box at 40 dollars per box, totaling 800 dollars.\\n\\nFor toothpaste, she took ten packs of six tubes each at 53 dollars per pack, totaling 530 dollars.\\n\\nWhen her eyes landed on the pads on the shelf, she overheard two employees walking nearby, whispering to each other.\\n\\n\\\"What's going on today? All these empty shelves?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Beats me. I could have sworn we were fully stocked this morning. How did it sell out so fast?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Probably another delivery crew. So annoying.\\\"\\n\\nJuliet smiled at the conversation and headed to the produce section on the first floor. With the employees restocking there, she figured it was best to hold off on looting for a bit.\\n\\nThe first floor was still mostly empty. The fruits were sold by the box and stacked against the walls, each variety with over a hundred boxes. Juliet took 30 boxes of each.\\n\\nAll she had to do was place her hand on an item while no one was looking during her selection, and it would vanish into her pocket dimension in the blink of an eye. Even the sharpest eyes would think they were seeing things.\\n\\nAnd Juliet, having fought alone in the apocalypse for years, had top-tier observation skills. There was no way anyone would catch on.\\n\\nPeaches, 17 dollars per box. 30 boxes, 510 dollars.\\n\\nWatermelons, 20 dollars per box. 30 boxes, 600 dollars.\\n\\nBlueberries, 23 dollars per box. 30 boxes, 690 dollars.\\n\\nKiwis, 100 dollars per box. 30 boxes, 3,000 dollars.\\n\\nCherries, 67 dollars per box. 30 boxes, 2,010 dollars.\\n\\nApples, 35 dollars per box. 30 boxes, 1,050 dollars.\\n\\nAvocados, 30 dollars per box. 30 boxes, 900 dollars.\\n\\nBy the time she left the fruit section, there wasn't much time left on the clock, but the alcohol aisle was nearby. Another great spot.\\n\\nHigh-end Hennessy was locked in glass cases, requiring purchase approval to take. But other brands like Michter's were fair game for anyone with money.\\n\\nRichard liked to have a drink when he had nothing to do, and alcohol could also disinfect wounds in a pinch. Absolutely essential for the apocalypse.\\n\\nJuliet swept through the aisle and grabbed over 13,000 dollars worth of liquor before finally feeling satisfied.\\n\\nWhen the hour ended, she had stocked a total of 134,416 dollars in supplies, earning her 7,680 square feet of pocket dimension space.\\n\\nShe tossed a few random items into her empty car for show and moved on to the next battlefield.\\n\\nSeaford was huge. There were several more locations of this big foreign membership wholesale club chain.\\n\\nAnd Juliet, the shopaholic she was in her previous life, happened to have memberships for all of them. She was going to hit every single one and spread the love equally.\\n\\nJuliet first hit another store for sanitary pads and birth control products. She wouldn't need the latter herself, but they would be great for trading later. Plenty of rich people would want those.\\n\\nThen she kept hoarding rice, flour, oil, salt, water, and seasonings, the most basic and most important supplies.\\n\\nShe left home at 8 AM and came back at 8 PM. A full day of looting, and she had grabbed one million dollars worth of supplies.\\n\\nThe high-end clubs marked up their prices, but the quality was solid. And the system converted based on the sticker price, so she wasn't losing anything.\\n\\nWith tens of thousands of pounds of food sitting in her pocket dimension, Juliet felt safer than she had in years.\\n\\nWhen she got home, Ryan and the others had already been back for a while. They were worried about Juliet and were about to go pick her up.\\n\\nRichard and Monica had hit up a few markets, buying 200 pounds of pork and 100 pounds of eggs from each one. They also grabbed potatoes, broccoli, and pasta, things that stored well and didn't draw too much attention.\\n\\nRyan had brought someone over to measure the rooftop and scheduled the glass room and steel mesh installation for a week later.\\n\\n\\\"You were gone all day and that's all you brought back?\\\" Ryan leaned sideways on the couch, eyeing the four plastic bags in her hands with obvious disgust.\\n\\nJuliet rolled her eyes at him and plopped down on the other end of the couch. \\\"What do you know? Don't flash your cash. I put everything in the pocket dimension.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"How much did you spend? I'll cover it.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Don't bother. I looted it all.\\\"\\n\\nTo show him what real skill looked like, Juliet casually demonstrated what she meant by looting. The car keys vanished from her hand and reappeared in the blink of an eye. Ryan and his parents watched, their expressions stiffening.\\n\\n\\\"Juliet, isn't it wrong to take things without paying?\\\" Monica asked after a long hesitation.\\n\\nJuliet had expected this. Under normal circumstances, what she did was theft, not just wrong but criminal. Based on what she had looted today, she would be looking at life in prison. But things were different now.\\n\\n\\\"Mom, I know what you're worried about. But even if I didn't take this stuff, it'd all be underwater in a month. Ordinary people won't be able to go outside and collect supplies.\\n\\n\\\"I'm sure the apocalypse is coming early. I just want to get us stocked up for the next few decades before it hits, without anyone finding out. Nothing else matters.\\\"\\n\\nChapter 5 My Daughter Won't Go Without Anything\\nWhen Juliet mentioned \\\"a few years from now\\\" again, Richard and Monica's faces darkened.\\n\\nThey stayed quiet for a moment, dropped the looting topic, and started asking about the apocalypse instead.\\n\\n\\\"We live by the coast. If things get that bad, won't there be tsunamis? And if that happens, where are we supposed to go?\\\"\\n\\nRichard's two houses weren't far from the ocean. The complex was perched on a hill with the mountains to its left, the water in front, and the main road on the right. Prime location. The crown jewel of the whole development.\\n\\nThe building had 30 floors, and they were on the top floor. A row of villas stood between the complex and the water, offering completely unobstructed, million-dollar views every single day.\\n\\nWaking up to that view would put anyone in a good mood. To be honest, Richard really didn't want to leave.\\n\\n\\\"There will be tsunamis, but from what I know, that's at least 15 years away,\\\" Juliet said.\\n\\nThat morning, Juliet hadn't had time to tell them much. She only covered the natural disasters in the first three years.\\n\\nNow after a full day to let it sink in, they had accepted that the apocalypse was coming, and their mental walls were stronger now.\\n\\n\\\"The first three years will bring flooding. But you know how high our complex sits. The first floor here is as high as the fifth floor in other complexes. The floods won't reach us.\\n\\n\\\"The building was still standing more than a decade later, converted into an alliance base. Beyond that, I don't know.\\\"\\n\\nRichard nodded. Fifteen years was good enough for him.\\n\\n\\\"Juliet, tell us more about the zombies you mentioned this morning,\\\" Monica said. \\\"Did anyone ever develop a cure?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"No.\\\" Juliet sighed.\\n\\n\\\"And this is something I need you all to remember. We can't stay home forever. One day, we'll have to go outside and fight. Do not let them scratch or bite you. Once you're infected, there's no saving you.\\\"\\n\\nRichard looked down at his beer belly and made a decision. Starting tomorrow, he was going to lose the weight. No way was he dragging Juliet down.\\n\\nMonica's face creased with worry. \\\"Prescription drugs and antibiotics will be hard to come by, won't they?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"All drugs will be hard to come by. Even if there are any left, the people at the top will control them. So, Mom, this is what I meant when I said we need to spend smart.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"You used to be a surgeon. You know exactly what we'll need if a plague hits. Buy it now. All of it. Don't worry about expiration dates. Our pocket dimension keeps everything exactly the way it was the moment it goes in.\\\"\\n\\nMonica finally let herself smile, a little relieved.\\n\\nThis whole thing was insane for someone Monica's age. It sounded like pure science fiction. More than once that day, she had asked herself if she was dreaming. But in the end, she chose to believe Juliet.\\n\\n'Even if it costs everything we have,' Monica thought, 'I won't let my daughter go without anything in the apocalypse. I'll just go along for this wild ride. Money can be made again. I only have one daughter.'\\n\\n\\\"Mom, I need you to help me figure out where the hospitals and quarantine stations keep their drug supplies.\\n\\n\\\"The stuff we can buy at regular stores is just the tip of the iceberg. There are high-value drugs like anesthetics and vaccines we can't get our hands on normally.\\n\\n\\\"On the last night before the apocalypse, when the riots start, I'm going to hit those places and clean them out.\\n\\n\\\"We also need a full set of surgical instruments. If someone gets hurt down the line, you'll be ready to go to work, just like old times.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Got it. I'll go buy medicine tomorrow.\\\" Monica agreed without hesitation.\\n\\nThe mood was still heavy. Ryan turned on the TV.\\n\\nJuliet had already stashed her own TV that morning, so Ryan brought the one from next door.\\n\\nThe local news was on, covering the old men who had died on the side of the road last night.\\n\\nThe family watched closely, then kept talking about the zombies.\\n\\n\\\"How soon do you turn after getting bitten?\\\" Ryan asked.\\n\\n\\\"The virus is weak at first. Takes three to seven days. Later on, it gets nasty. So, ten seconds.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"The only way to take them out is the same as in the movies? Headshots?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Yeah, headshots. Nothing else works.\\\"\\n\\nRyan clicked his tongue, troubled.\\n\\n\\\"We can't get guns, and even if we could, we'd never become sharpshooters. I need to find some crossbows and arrows.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Guns are possible. Things will get chaotic, and people will sell them. When the apocalypse first hits, I can grab a few while everything's falling apart. Crossbows work too. Besides those...\\\" Juliet turned to look at Richard, who hadn't said a word.\\n\\nRichard grew up in the countryside. He had a hot temper when he was young, pushing a fruit cart on the streets, getting into arguments left and right.\\n\\nHe had always loved bladed weapons. To this day, he kept a machete under his pillow, just in case some thug ever broke into the house.\\n\\n\\\"Dad, do you have connections to get good-quality straight swords? They work great on zombies.\\\"\\n\\nRichard's eyes lit up. \\\"I do. Wait here. I'll get you some tomorrow. No, I'll make some calls right now.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"As many as you can find. As fast as possible. It'd be perfect if we could get them within a week. The apocalypse could hit early. We need to be ready.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Got it.\\\"\\n\\nRichard grabbed his phone and stepped next door to make calls. Monica watched him go and shook her head with a small smile.\\n\\n\\\"You bring up weapons and your dad wakes right up. You know how much he loves this stuff. Every movie or show he watches at home is all action and fighting.\\\"\\n\\nThat reminded Juliet of something. She needed to get a bunch of external hard drives and download movies, TV shows, anime, variety programs, anything to watch when they got bored later.\\n\\nRichard liked action flicks. Monica liked period dramas about royal harems. Juliet and Ryan liked mostly the same things, anime, horror, thrillers, anything.\\n\\n\\\"Ryan, do you know anyone who sells hard drives? Let's grab some and download everything while the internet is still up.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"How long until the networks go down?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Three months after the apocalypse starts, max. At first, people still had hope. They thought everything could be contained.\\n\\n\\\"But the zombies evolved faster and faster. Even the military got overrun. The networks went down too, either from human sabotage or natural disasters.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Let's go get the drives now.\\\" Ryan was a man of action. He grabbed his keys and headed for the door, scrolling through his contacts as he walked. He worked in software and knew plenty of people who sold electronics.\\n\\nMonica watched them leave, worry written all over her face. \\\"Ryan, take care of your sister.\\\"\\n\\nChapter 6 Apocalypse Farming and Ranching System Unlocked\\nRyan drove them to the electronics store. The biggest hard drives they carried were 20TB each, priced at 800 dollars a pop.\\n\\nHe didn't even blink. He asked how many they had left and bought 30 of them.\\n\\nNormally, a 1TB drive could hold about 100 movies. A 20TB drive could hold 2,000. But if they wanted high-definition Blu-ray quality, each movie would take up 30 to 50GB. A 1TB drive would only fit 20 to 30 movies at that rate.\\n\\nAnyway, 30 drives would be plenty for now.\\n\\nWhen they got home, Ryan and Juliet fired up every computer in the house. They spent a small fortune on premium subscriptions for a few download services and started downloading movies and TV shows overnight.\\n\\nRyan sat in the study, unable to sleep. Juliet slipped into the pocket dimension while the computers did their thing.\\n\\nShe had a bit of OCD. Leaving all those supplies piled up in a messy heap bothered her. Plus she had been busy all day and hadn't gotten a good look at her upgraded space yet.\\n\\nAfter a full day of work, her little thatched hut had transformed into a three-story luxury villa with a basement. Even the garden plot in front of the villa had expanded quite a bit.\\n\\nJuliet started planning. Since the system had family member slots, that meant Richard and Ryan could come in here too one day.\\n\\nThe basement had a media room. The family could sing karaoke and watch movies down there without worrying about bothering the neighbors.\\n\\nShe had a few other rooms next to the media room. She would use one for alcohol, one for trade goods like cigarettes and coffee beans, and the last one for convenience foods, such as ramen, Spam, and canned food.\\n\\nShe could cook fresh meals in the pocket dimension now, but there would be times when she had to go out and deal with other people. She would need to keep up appearances.\\n\\nThe first floor had a living room, a kitchen, a dining area, and three bedrooms, each with its own bathroom. Perfect for a family of four.\\n\\nThe second floor had three rooms for clothes, shoes, toilet paper, pads, toothpaste, and other daily necessities. The remaining rooms would hold books and valuable antiques and artwork she would collect later.\\n\\nOn the third floor, Juliet planned to turn one room into an operating room. The rest would store medical supplies and equipment.\\n\\nEverything else would go in the backyard for now.\\n\\nBehind the villa was a yard about 1,000 square feet. Unlike the garden out front, this one was paved with concrete.\\n\\nJuliet planned to put up the kind of heavy-duty shelving they had in supermarkets, organize everything by category, and label the shelves for easy access.\\n\\nIf the pocket dimension kept expanding, she could just throw entire shipping containers in here.\\n\\nAfter roughing out the plan, Juliet got to work.\\n\\nShe soon discovered that she could move objects with her mind. But she had only gotten this pocket dimension recently and wasn't good at it yet. After about five minutes of using her powers, her vision would blur and her head would spin.\\n\\nShe switched between telekinesis and manual labor. Three hours of organizing inside the pocket dimension, and she had finally worked through most of the supplies she had looted that day.\\n\\nJust as she was about to step out and grab more stuff, the system gave her a new quest.\\n\\n\\\"Ding. Apocalypse Farming and Ranching System unlocked.\\\"\\n\\n[Within 24 hours, complete the following: Live chickens (0/10), live ducks (0/10), live geese (0/10), live pigs (0/3), live goats (0/3), live cattle (0/3). Reward: One fish pond.]\\n\\nJuliet raised an eyebrow, a smile playing on her lips. 'A fish pond? Does that mean we'll have fresh fish and shrimp whenever we want? Good system. You totally get a foodie.'\\n\\nShe hurried out of the pocket dimension and told Ryan and the others.\\n\\nThe next morning after breakfast, they split up again. Monica went out to buy medicine, Richard and Juliet headed to buy livestock, and Ryan set off to find crossbows and arrows.\\n\\nThere was a decent-sized livestock trading market on the outskirts of Seaford. Richard drove straight there.\\n\\nHe had wanted to come alone and spare Juliet the smell, but she pointed out that he couldn't haul everything back in one trip by himself, and with her along, it would be easier, so he gave in.\\n\\nRichard was a rough guy, but he had grown up in the countryside. He had farmed land, raised pigs, and sold all kinds of goods as a street vendor. He knew his way around a market.\\n\\nTo avoid drawing attention, he had even rented a box truck overnight.\\n\\nAt the poultry section, Richard picked out ten free-range chickens, each about two pounds. Eight hens and two roosters. He also grabbed five guinea fowl.\\n\\nHe bought sixteen ducks in total. Following a proper breeding ratio, there were fourteen female ducks and two drakes.\\n\\nNext were twelve geese: ten female geese and two ganders.\\n\\nHe also got three pigs: one boar and two sows.\\n\\nThen three sheep: one ram and two ewes.\\n\\nAnd three cattle: one bull and two cows.\\n\\nRichard spotted some rabbits and grabbed two of those too. They were easy to raise and bred like crazy. Even if every other animal died on them, they could still eat rabbits.\\n\\nHe also bought two mules. That stuff was unbelievably delicious. And even if they didn't eat them, the mules could pull a millstone. He would put them to work grinding grain.\\n\\nThe total came to 25,000 dollars.\\n\\nThe seller looked at Richard and Juliet's clothes and figured they weren't in the livestock business. He asked them what they needed all these animals for.\\n\\nRichard smiled and said, \\\"Opening a farm-to-table place. You know, a restaurant where people can feed the animals too. Can't afford the fancy stuff, so I'm just getting the cheap ones. Kids in the city haven't seen any of this before.\\\"\\n\\nThe seller nodded along. \\\"Yeah, that'll do it.\\\"\\n\\nAfter they finished talking, Richard had the seller's guys help load everything onto the truck. He drove to a deserted spot with no cameras. Juliet moved the livestock into her pocket dimension and completed the quest.\\n\\nWith the fish pond now theirs, they drove straight to the seafood market next.\\n\\nCarp, rainbow trout, catfish, pike, sunfish, salmon, sea bass. They bought a bunch of every variety.\\n\\nRichard had only ever kept ornamental fish before. He didn't know much about raising edible ones, so he asked the seller a ton of questions.\\n\\nJuliet stood there listening, occasionally looking around to see what else they might need. Her eyes stopped on a stall selling saltwater seafood.\\n\\nA few years from now, the global ocean system would be contaminated by nuclear wastewater. Not just crabs and shrimp, even seaweed wouldn't be safe to eat.\\n\\nRichard turned around and saw Juliet staring blankly at the seafood stall. He walked over. \\\"What's wrong, sweetie? You craving something? I'll buy it for you.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"No. I was just thinking about what a shame this is.\\\"\\n\\nJuliet leaned in and whispered to Richard about the ocean contamination that was coming. His brow furrowed when he heard it.\\n\\nRichard had always figured that living right by the ocean meant they would never run out of seafood. He hadn't counted on nuclear wastewater.\\n\\nHe muttered a curse under his breath, then patted Juliet on the head. \\\"I'll buy some and raise them for you at home.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Forget it. You can't keep them alive. Saltwater fish need regular water changes. We won't have that kind of setup later.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"We'll keep them alive as long as we can. When they die, we'll just eat them.\\\"\\n\\nRichard wasn't discouraged at all. He walked up to the seafood stall and started chatting with the seller.\\n\\nIn the end, he bought out the seller's entire stock of saltwater seafood and took the glass fish tank too. He was dead serious about learning how to raise seafood at home for Juliet.\\n\\nChapter 7 Running into the Feuding Neighbors\\nAfter spending 17,000 dollars at the seafood market, Richard and Juliet drove off.\\n\\nOn the way back, they talked about Juliet's pocket dimension. When Richard found out she could farm inside it too, he immediately found a place to buy seeds and saplings.\\n\\nCilantro, cabbage, eggplant, persimmons, chili peppers, carrots, celery, green onions, lettuce, romaine, spinach. They grabbed 100 packs of each of the common varieties.\\n\\nApple, cherry, fig, plum, blueberry, strawberry, grape, pear, peach, apricot. Ten saplings of each type of fruit tree.\\n\\nAfter spending another 10,000 dollars, they hit the home improvement store again.\\n\\nThey bought ten high-capacity solar batteries, ten solar panels, 20 portable solar-powered night lights, five gas generators, and five diesel generators.\\n\\nAll that came to 67,000 dollars. Richard swiped his card without flinching and turned to ask Juliet if she needed anything else.\\n\\n\\\"We're good for now. Let's head back,\\\" Juliet said.\\n\\nWhen they got home, Ryan and Monica had just returned too. They all shared what they had bought that day.\\n\\nRyan had bought ten satellite phones at 2,000 dollars each and 10 SIM cards at 35 dollars each. Each card gave him 300 minutes of talk time, but the clock only started running once activated.\\n\\nAn unactivated card stayed good for seven years, while an activated one lasted one year. He hadn't dared buy too many.\\n\\nRyan had also asked a friend to find him four crossbows and 1,000 arrows. He was scheduled to pick them up tomorrow.\\n\\nMonica had hit up several hospitals and pharmacies and bought about 13,000 dollars worth of common medications.\\n\\nShe had also gone to a few markets farther from home and bought 100 pounds of sausage and 100 pounds of eggs at each one.\\n\\nShe didn't shop near the neighborhood. Too many familiar faces. Some of their neighbors even ran stalls at those markets. If word got out, when the apocalypse hit, those people would come banging down their door.\\n\\n\\\"Some medications have purchase limits. I couldn't get much,\\\" Monica said. \\\"But online pharmacies are still open. We can order more there.\\\"\\n\\nJuliet smiled and nodded, glad that the whole family working together got things done fast.\\n\\nThey all pulled out their phones and started shopping, choosing next-day delivery for everything.\\n\\nAfter Juliet finished ordering medicine, she bought 10,000 heat packs. Those would be gold on subzero days outside.\\n\\nThe heat packs came in packs of ten. She ordered 50 packs from each store and had them shipped to a 24-hour self-service parcel locker in a nearby neighborhood.\\n\\nAs long as one didn't grab anyone else's packages, no one ever checked the cameras to see how many they picked up. Quick and easy.\\n\\nThey grabbed a bite at home, then headed out again. This time, they went shopping for clothes and shoes.\\n\\nMost of Juliet and Monica's current wardrobe was dresses and fashion sneakers. They didn't have many pairs of real athletic shoes.\\n\\nAt the mall, each of them picked out combat boots and tactical jackets, then grabbed lightweight pants, followed by thermal underwear, cashmere sweaters, and down jackets.\\n\\nIt was summer, so winter gear was on clearance. Plenty of people were shopping for deals. No one suspected anything.\\n\\nWhen they finished, Juliet and Monica carried their bags into the restroom, the only place in the mall without cameras. Juliet quickly stashed everything in her pocket dimension.\\n\\nOn the way out, Juliet spotted a few sporting goods stores. Her eyes lit up. She dragged Ryan, her human credit card, inside and grabbed baseball bats, helmets, protective gear, camping tents, and sleeping bags.\\n\\nWhile swiping Ryan's card, she couldn't help asking, \\\"Ryan, you've only been working a few years. You must've almost blown your whole salary by now, huh?\\\"\\n\\nRyan let out a cold laugh and shot her a sideways look. \\\"You're forgiven for your ignorance this time.\\\"\\n\\nJuliet pouted. She planned to keep using his card. After all, she was a broke college student who hadn't graduated yet.\\n\\nRyan handed all the gear to Juliet and told her to take another trip to the restroom to stash it.\\n\\nThen the whole family headed to the basement supermarket. Each of them grabbed a cart and started stocking up on daily necessities and food.\\n\\nJuliet kept looting in places where no one was watching. She cleared out all the ramen, pasta, mashed potatoes, stew sauce, soup packets and more.\\n\\nBy the time they left, on top of what Ryan and the others had paid for, Juliet had stashed a few hundred boxes of instant food, 500 pounds of rice, 300 pounds of flour, and over 1,000 bottles of water.\\n\\nBy night, the whole family headed home with their haul. On the way back from the underground garage, they ran into their downstairs neighbors, the Zuniga family.\\n\\nJuliet's face darkened the moment she saw them.\\n\\nIan Zuniga and his wife Irene used to work at the same hospital as Monica. Their son Ethan was Juliet's age. They had gone to the same preschool, elementary school, middle school, and high school together.\\n\\nWhen the Venns bought their place in this development, Monica had already quit the hospital.\\n\\nMonica figured it was good to have connections at the hospital in case her family got sick.\\n\\nSo when the Zunigas moved into the unit below and started hinting that they wanted Juliet to marry Ethan after graduation and move into the house the Venns had bought for her, Monica didn't say much.\\n\\nShe didn't want Juliet to leave home anyway. If Juliet and Ethan actually fell for each other and got married, living in her house wouldn't be a problem.\\n\\nAnyway, they knew each other's families. With Monica and Richard nearby, Juliet wouldn't get mistreated after marriage.\\n\\nBut everything depended on Juliet. If she wasn't interested, the Zunigas' hints meant nothing.\\n\\n\\\"Hey, Richard. Where did you folks go?\\\" Ian and his family walked over smiling.\\n\\nAll the stuff the Venns had bought was already in Juliet's pocket dimension, so Richard just replied, \\\"We went out to eat. Too hot to cook at home. What about you?\\\"\\n\\nIan shook the big bag in his hand. \\\"Had dinner at my dad's place. Here, look what they gave us.\\\"\\n\\nEthan saw Juliet and couldn't take his eyes off her. He strode over. \\\"Juliet, let's go to a movie tomorrow. I'll take you to dinner after.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"No thanks. I'm busy.\\\" Juliet's voice was cold.\\n\\n\\\"Busy doing what? Shopping for clothes, right? I can come with you.\\\" Ethan brushed it off and reached for her hand. \\\"I know this super expensive restaurant with amazing food. My treat.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Really, I'm good. I have actual things to do.\\\" Juliet dodged his hand and grabbed Ryan's arm. \\\"I'm going out with my brother tomorrow. Ask him if you don't believe me.\\\"\\n\\nRyan saw how his sister was reacting and knew she didn't like this guy, so he played along. \\\"Yeah. We've got plans.\\\"\\n\\nEthan didn't push it. They all walked into the elevator together. At the 29th floor, the Zuniga family got off first.\\n\\nThe moment they walked through their door, Irene rolled her eyes. \\\"That little brat Juliet. What's she so cocky about? Ethan's been chasing her for ages and she still won't say yes. If her family didn't have money, I wouldn't even want her.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Mom, don't worry,\\\" Ethan said. \\\"Once she marries me, both her family's houses are ours. And that Cayenne her dad bought her will be perfect for my commute.\\\"\\n\\nChapter 8 The Apocalypse Makes Everyone A Villain\\nThe Zuniga family had it all figured out on paper. Too bad Juliet had already seen right through them.\\n\\nThe moment she got home and the door clicked shut, Juliet dropped onto the couch. \\\"Mom, Dad, remember when I said our neighbors would eventually come for us?\\\"\\n\\nThey both nodded.\\n\\n\\\"I didn't tell you who started it back then. It was the Zuniga family downstairs. Irene came up first, crying her eyes out, saying they had nothing to eat and were about to starve.\\n\\n\\\"We thought we were neighbors and would be seeing each other all the time anyway. If we helped each other out, we might be able to get through this. But they didn't see it that way.\\\"\\n\\nJuliet let out a cold laugh, remembering the Zunigas' playbook.\\n\\n\\\"We gave them food the first and second day. But by the third day, we were running low ourselves, and we could tell something was off about the whole thing. So we said no.\\n\\n\\\"Irene went straight to the neighborhood group and told everyone we had tons of supplies but were letting people die. All those neighbors who were already half-starved and out of their minds went crazy. They banded together and stormed our place.\\n\\n\\\"We weren't prepared, and there were too many of them. They smashed the door and the windows.\\n\\n\\\"They broke in and took the last crumbs of food we had. But the Zunigas still weren't happy. They had their eyes on our two houses the whole time.\\n\\n\\\"And here's the kicker. They had 100 pounds of rice sitting in their own place the whole time. They just didn't want to eat it. So they were never desperate.\\\"\\n\\nJuliet laid it all out like she was talking about the weather. Richard was so mad he started cursing right there on the spot.\\n\\n\\\"Son of a bitch. They look decent, but they're goddamn animals underneath. I knew that family was no good. That ugly son of theirs thinks he's something, always panting after my daughter.\\\"\\n\\nMonica's face had gone dark too. She sat in silence for a moment, then turned to Richard. \\\"Honey, you called about the swords yesterday. What did they say? When can we get them?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Tomorrow. I'll go grab them first thing in the morning. Anyone dares to start trouble at our door, I'll put a hole right through them.\\\" Richard was breathing hard, ready to march downstairs and start a fight right then and there.\\n\\nRyan was calm, but the look in his eyes said plenty.\\n\\n\\\"I'm not telling you this to make you mad,\\\" Juliet said. \\\"I'm giving you a heads-up. When the apocalypse hits, everyone turns into a villain. If we go soft, other people will kill us. So we need to be ready.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"You're right, sweetie.\\\" Richard nodded along, already scrolling on his phone, looking for more weapons.\\n\\nRyan turned to Juliet. \\\"How much more can your pocket dimension hold? Is there a limit?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"For now, about 350 dollars of supplies buys me ten square feet. I have a 10,000-square-foot villa now, plus a plot of farmland and another 1,000 square feet of open space. The system will probably give me more later.\\\"\\n\\nShe wasn't hiding anything. She planned to bring them all in eventually.\\n\\n\\\"The system gives me random quests. I only have one family member slot right now, but if the pocket dimension can let one family member go in with me now, that means you and dad will probably be able to get in later.\\n\\n\\\"Then you two can go check out what it's like inside.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Got it.\\\" Ryan wasn't one to be dramatic about things. \\\"I'll come see your fancy villa when the time comes.\\\"\\n\\nAfter breakfast the next morning, Richard went out to pick up the swords, while Ryan was off somewhere doing God knows what.\\n\\n\\\"Juliet, come with me to your grandparents' old house,\\\" Monica said. \\\"I want to bring back the things they left behind.\\\"\\n\\nJuliet's grandfather had been a traditional medicine doctor. He and his wife died in a car accident, leaving behind shelves of books and herbs.\\n\\nHe had wanted Monica to take over the family practice, but she went behind his back and studied modern medicine instead. The old man nearly had a heart attack.\\n\\nLater, he set his sights on Ryan and Juliet. Ryan never had any interest in that stuff.\\n\\nAs for Juliet, Ryan had gotten to her first. From the time she was little, he had her watching CSI, Dexter, Bones, anything about forensics. He was determined to turn his little sister into a great medical examiner.\\n\\nWhen Juliet was ten, Ryan got caught red-handed by Monica when she came home from a hospital night shift. He got a good beating and never tried to fool Juliet again.\\n\\nThinking back to Ryan at 15, still being chased around the house by Monica and howling, Juliet almost laughed out loud.\\n\\nJuliet drove Monica to the old house. Dust covered every surface. Three floor-to-ceiling bookshelves were crammed full.\\n\\nThey cleaned the place up, then Juliet stashed the bookshelves in her pocket dimension. They went through the medicine cabinets and grabbed any herbs that were still usable.\\n\\n\\\"Juliet, you said modern medicine will be hard to find later. What about traditional medicine?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Some of it, but not much. The climate goes crazy. The wild areas outside become death traps. Zombies are everywhere, plus gangs of murderers and thieves running around.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Can you grow medicinal herbs in your pocket dimension?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Theoretically, yeah. But I've never done it, so...\\\"\\n\\n\\\"That's fine. I'll figure it out.\\\" Monica sighed. \\\"I should've listened to your grandfather and learned more from him when I had the chance.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"It's not too late. We're prepared this time. I'll get some surgical equipment too. When modern medicine works, it works faster.\\\"\\n\\nMonica nodded. That was exactly why she had chosen modern medicine back then. Traditional methods took too long.\\n\\nThey spent the day looting. When they got home that night, Richard had already picked up what Juliet wanted.\\n\\nJuliet's eyes went wide when she saw five straight swords lying on the couch. She lunged at them. \\\"This many?\\\" She had thought getting one would be a win.\\n\\nRichard sat nearby, watching Juliet's face light up, a smug look on his face as he crossed his legs. \\\"That's what they call money making the world go round.\\\"\\n\\nEach sword cost 13,000 dollars, so five of them came to 65,000 dollars.\\n\\nRichard had been a nervous wreck driving home, though. Every time he saw a cop, he held his breath. Getting pulled over with five swords in the car would have been a nightmare to explain.\\n\\nWith weapons in hand, Juliet felt a lot more secure.\\n\\nOver the next few days, they kept going out and looting.\\n\\nJuliet brought home several sets of workout equipment. She was going to make sure Ryan and the others got into shape.\\n\\nRyan bought ten assault boats, plus rafts and life jackets. Juliet didn't dare ask how much they cost. She just shoved them into her pocket dimension and watched the space visibly expand.\\n\\nA week of nonstop work passed. The day finally came for the glass room installation on the rooftop.\\n\\nJuliet supervised along with Ryan. When she saw the finished product, the dimensions Ryan had measured himself, the design he had come up with, she couldn't help but think her brother was a badass.\\n\\nChapter 9 Apocalypse Defense System Unlocked\\nWhen Ryan first mentioned installing a sunroom and steel mesh, Juliet assumed the mesh would go on the outside of the glass. That was how everyone did it. But Ryan had the vendor put it on the inside instead.\\n\\nJuliet moved closer to him and lowered her voice. \\\"Why is the steel mesh on the inside?\\\"\\n\\nRyan answered like it was obvious. \\\"You said the weather is going to get bad later. I was worried about acid rain eating through it.\\\"\\n\\nJuliet didn't say anything, but Ryan could tell from the look on her face that he had guessed right.\\n\\nShe sighed in awe. She didn't want to admit it, but her brother had been smart since he was a kid. That was the only reason MIT had recruited him.\\n\\nThe noise from the installation quickly caught the attention of the building security. A guard called Richard and told him this was an illegal structure. Richard cursed under his breath but kept smiling as he answered.\\n\\n\\\"Then let the neighbors report it. Once the complaint goes through, I'll take it down. Don't put yourself in a tough spot. You go report it too.\\\"\\n\\nThe guard hung up looking confused. He couldn't tell whether Richard was being serious or not. Left with no choice, he did what Richard said.\\n\\nBut complaints took time. The whole process would take at least half a month to go through. By then, nobody knew if anyone would still be around to enforce it.\\n\\nMost of the day passed before the sunroom was finished.\\n\\nThe roof had manually operated honeycomb blinds. The south side had several sliding glass windows for ventilation in the summer.\\n\\nOn that same side, hidden above the windows, a roll of high-transparency steel mesh could be pulled down from a box to keep out bugs.\\n\\nJust open the windows, grab the edge from the top frame, pull it down, and lock it into the bottom frame.\\n\\nRyan had paid an extra 3,500 dollars for that little design feature.\\n\\nJuliet was admiring the finished sunroom when the installation crew walked out the door. The next second, the system voice spoke in her head again after what felt like forever.\\n\\n\\\"Ding. Apocalypse Defense System unlocked.\\\"\\n\\n[Congratulations on completing the defense upgrade quest. Reward: Defense system upgraded. Pocket dimension temperature now permanent. Reward: One defense technician slot.]\\n\\nJuliet blinked. She hadn't even realized where a defense technician would come from, but the next second, she saw the sunroom change right in front of her eyes.\\n\\nThe glass thickness had changed. It was now professional-grade bulletproof. They could see out but no one could see in. And not just the sunroom glass. The windows in their apartment had all been upgraded too.\\n\\n\\\"Shit.\\\" Ryan's voice came from the front door. He rarely cursed, so Juliet ran over to see what was wrong.\\n\\n\\\"What happened?\\\" Ryan pointed at her front door when he saw Juliet.\\n\\nHe had been about to install some security cameras when the door suddenly changed right in front of him. It scared the hell out of him.\\n\\nJuliet looked at her new front door, now bank-vault-grade, and laughed out loud. Then she looked at what Ryan was holding and realized who the defense technician was supposed to be.\\n\\nJuliet grabbed Ryan and pulled him close. In the blink of an eye, they were both inside the pocket dimension.\\n\\nRyan's heart started pounding as the scene in front of him suddenly shifted.\\n\\nHe stood there frozen for a second, catching his breath, then looked around. \\\"So this is... your pocket dimension?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Yeah. Let me bind you to the system first. Take a look around.\\\"\\n\\nA panel appeared in front of Juliet, invisible to Ryan, displaying all of his information.\\n\\nName: Ryan Venn\\n\\nGender: Male\\n\\nAge: 24\\n\\nHeight: 6'1\\\"\\n\\nBlood Type: B\\n\\nProfession: Software Engineer\\n\\nRelationship: Brother\\n\\nLoyalty: Never Betray\\n\\nJuliet quickly linked Ryan's info and went to find him.\\n\\nRyan was already inside the villa, checking out the bedrooms Juliet had set up. He nodded, satisfied. At least his sister had some decency and wasn't making him sleep in a doghouse.\\n\\n\\\"Not bad, right?\\\" Juliet leaned against the doorframe and pointed at his bed. \\\"An electric bed. Cost over 13,000 dollars.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Not bad.\\\" Ryan walked over and flicked her on the forehead. \\\"Don't let anyone outside the family know about this pocket dimension. Or someone will blow your stupid head off.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Relax. I know.\\\"\\n\\nJuliet knew better than he did how nasty the apocalypse could be.\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"SEE DETAILS\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://website.novelbars.com/share/middle_new/tvr0hfjhfeots35jkvos13cr?campaign_id={{campaign.id}}&adset_id={{adset.id}}&ad_id={{ad.id}}&campaign={{campaign.name}}&adgroup={{adset.name}}\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"website.novelbars.com\",\"ctaDescription\":\"😍What happens next👉Click Here to read on👉\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":null},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":462,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":4,\"duplicates\":172,\"reachDelta1d\":null,\"reachDelta7d\":null,\"reachDelta30d\":null},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":[\"AU\",\"CA\",\"GB\",\"NZ\",\"US\"],\"mainCountry\":\"GB\",\"gender\":\"women\",\"ageMin\":18,\"ageMax\":65},\"rank\":{\"positionInPage\":46,\"currentRank\":46,\"rankDelta\":null,\"improvementPct\":5.38},\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":true,\"isLowReach\":true,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}},{\"id\":\"facebook_1013238867965501\",\"collationId\":\"973037255532183\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"inactive\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-06-16T01:15:11.505Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-06-15T07:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-07-27T07:00:00.000Z\",\"daysRunning\":42,\"media\":{\"type\":\"image\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/image/e9d7d181f531dbd6e6fa9ae2c55b27a1b20b216ad8d7656e4371a960b90dfcf0.jpg\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"824855830705111\",\"name\":\"Auri Labs\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/profile_picture/824855830705111.