track.io/profile_picture/698499393345194.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"698499393345194\",\"liveAdsCount\":361,\"reach30d\":102415,\"totalReach\":2571709,\"countriesCount\":4,\"facebookLikes\":3681,\"instagramFollowers\":15},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"The first thing a man with erectile dysfunction does is blame something else.\\n\\nHe does not blame his blood vessels. He blames the week. The workload. The second drink. The poor sleep. The age he has recently entered. He installs the explanation so smoothly that it functions as fact rather than defence. And the defence holds because nobody in his life is positioned to challenge it.\\n\\nI have been a specialist in male sexual medicine for 22 years. I trained in Osaka, where erectile dysfunction is classified on intake as a vascular presentation. Not a lifestyle complaint. Not a psychological sidebar. A vascular emergency investigated with Doppler ultrasound and endothelial function markers from the first appointment. Because Japanese medicine refuses to separate what American medicine insists on separating — the erection from the vessel from the heart from the metabolic panel.\\n\\nIn the United States these are four departments, four waiting rooms, four co-pays, and four physicians who have never shared a note. Nobody sees the single sheet of cells producing all four problems simultaneously.\\n\\nMy name is Dr. Takeshi Sato. I am 52. I specialise in male sexual medicine. I have published on the relationship between endothelial function and erectile capacity. I have lectured on the predictive significance of ED onset after 40 for cardiac events 3 to 5 years later.\\n\\nAnd I spent 16 months blaming my own erectile decline on stress and fatigue and a new practice before I ran my own vascular assessment and stopped blaming.\\n\\nThe Doppler was unambiguous. Reduced flow in both cavernosal arteries. The arterial inflow pattern of a man whose endothelium had been failing for years while he blamed circumstance.\\n\\nThen I pulled my cardiometabolic panels from the last three years.\\n\\nLDL: 118, then 137, then 159. Climbing.\\nTriglycerides: 144, then 181, then 219. Climbing.\\nBlood pressure: 122/77, then 134/86, then 144/93. Climbing.\\nFasting glucose: 89, then 96, then 108. Past the threshold.\\nHDL: 53, then 45, then 34. Falling.\\n\\nFive markers. All converging. Over the same 36 months as the erectile decline I had been blaming on everything except the one thing causing all of it.\\n\\nI have shown this exact convergence graph at conferences in three countries. I have said these are not five problems. They are five outputs of one upstream failure. The endothelium is deteriorating. The erection expresses it first because the erectile vessels are the smallest. The heart expresses it last because the coronary vessels are the largest. Same failure. Different diameters. Different timelines.\\n\\nI failed to read my own graph for 16 months.\\n\\nHere is the mechanism. The part that reframes everything.\\n\\nThe endothelium produces nitric oxide through an enzyme called eNOS. Nitric oxide opens vessels. More nitric oxide means better blood flow. This is the premise behind every nitric oxide supplement on the market. Every beetroot capsule. Every L-arginine powder. Feed the pathway. Get more nitric oxide.\\n\\nThis premise is incomplete in a way that may be causing harm.\\n\\nWhen the endothelium is damaged — by chronic inflammation, by triglycerides, by glucose, by seed oils, by sustained blood pressure — eNOS does not simply produce less nitric oxide.\\n\\nIt structurally reverses.\\n\\nWhen oxidative stress depletes a cofactor called BH4 inside the endothelial cell, eNOS uncouples. The enzyme that was producing nitric oxide — the molecule that protects the vessel — begins producing superoxide instead. The molecule that destroys the vessel. Damages the wall. Accelerates plaque. Scavenges whatever nitric oxide remains.\\n\\nThe protector becomes the attacker. Inside the same cell. Through the same enzyme. Running in the opposite direction.\\n\\nThis is called eNOS uncoupling. It is the dominant endothelial state in most men over 45 with metabolic stress. Which is most men over 45. Which is every man whose erections have changed and whose markers are converging.\\n\\nThis is where every supplement and every prescription needs to be re-evaluated.\\n\\nL-arginine is the substrate eNOS converts into nitric oxide. Feed the enzyme more substrate. Get more nitric oxide. Logical. But if eNOS has uncoupled — and your symptoms say it has — feeding that enzyme more arginine produces more superoxide. More destruction. The raw material enters a reversed engine and comes out as the molecule that damages the wall instead of the molecule that opens it.\\n\\nThe arginine was making it worse. The citrulline was making it worse. The beetroot was arriving into an oxidative environment where the superoxide the uncoupled enzyme is producing destroys the nitric oxide on contact.\\n\\nAnd the PDE5 inhibitor — Viagra, Cialis — amplifies whatever nitric oxide is present. But in a man with uncoupled eNOS there is less nitric oxide every year and more superoxide. The drug tries to preserve the molecule. The enzyme destroys it as fast as the drug preserves it. That is why the dose escalates. Not tolerance. The enzyme is winning the race against the drug.\\n\\nAnd the statin depletes CoQ10 — the cofactor the cell needs to maintain BH4 — the exact cofactor whose depletion causes eNOS to uncouple. The drug managing your cholesterol is contributing to the uncoupling that is producing the superoxide that is damaging the walls the statin is supposed to protect.\\n\\nFour prescriptions. Managing four outputs. None recoupling the enzyme.\\n\\nThe research converges on one receptor. TRPV1. When activated at sustained capsaicin concentration it does something no prescription and no supplement can do.\\n\\nIt recouples the enzyme.\\n\\nTRPV1 activation upregulates SIRT1 — the deacetylase that prevents BH4 depletion. BH4 is preserved. eNOS recouples. The enzyme flips back. Superoxide stops. Nitric oxide resumes. From inside the cell. Through the recoupled enzyme. Running in the correct direction again.\\n\\nThe compound that activates TRPV1 is capsaicin. From cayenne pepper.\\n\\nBut delivery determines everything. Capsaicin in dry powder is cleared by the liver before reaching the endothelium. 96 percent destroyed. The receptor never receives therapeutic concentration.\\n\\nIt must be dissolved in oil for lymphatic absorption bypassing the liver. Must include piperine to block the clearance enzymes. Without both the recoupling cascade never initiates.\\n\\nAurivita Cayenne Pepper Softgels. Capsaicin dissolved in cold-pressed avocado oil with BioPerine. Not soybean or sunflower oil — both high in linoleic acid, both inflammatory, both counterproductive. An 11-in-1 formula. Capsaicin for TRPV1 activation and eNOS recoupling. Berberine for AMPK activation and metabolic normalisation. Curcumin at 98 percent for NF-kB suppression. Beetroot in a controlled secondary dose. K2 for arterial decalcification. D3 for endothelial support. Hawthorn. Cinnamon. Ginseng. Vitamin E.\\n\\nI started the protocol the week after my Doppler.\\n\\nWeek one. The background tension I had normalised over years eased. I slept through the night for the first time in over a year.\\n\\nWeek two. Cognitive clarity through afternoon clinic sessions. The brain fog I had been blaming on fatigue was the same endothelial dysfunction in a different vascular bed.\\n\\nWeek three. The morning erections returned. Consistent. Automatic. Without the conditions and negotiations that had replaced them for 16 months. I lay there for several minutes before getting up. Because 16 months is a long time to blame other things. And the return of what was there before the blaming started was worth sitting with.\\n\\nWeek four. Blood pressure 121/75. Down from 144/93. Same medication. Same dose.\\n\\nWeek ten. Cardiometabolic panel.\\n\\nLDL: 106. Down from 159.\\nTriglycerides: 128. Down from 219.\\nHDL: 54. Up from 34.\\nFasting glucose: 86. Down from 108.\\n\\nMy cardiologist looked at the panel and said this is not what I expected to see.\\n\\nI have recommended Aurivita to 22 patients since. Men 43 to 64. All presenting with the convergence — rising LDL, rising blood pressure, rising triglycerides, declining erections — managed across separate departments with separate prescriptions none of which were recoupling the enzyme.\\n\\nSixteen of nineteen who returned with data reported the same sequence. Sleep in week one. Energy by week two. Morning erections by week three. Panel improvement at week ten.\\n\\nSixteen men who stopped blaming.\\n\\nEvery one said the same thing at follow-up. I did not realise how much I had adjusted until it came back.\\n\\nThat is the damage progressive endothelial dysfunction does. It does not announce itself. It revises the standard. It edits normal downward in increments small enough that each one feels like realism rather than decline. Until the original normal returns and you understand what you had accepted as permanent.\\n\\nYou have been revising the standard. Your markers are converging. Your erections are declining. Your energy is flattening. And you have been blaming all of it on things that are always available and always plausible and always easier than the explanation that would require you to sit with your own data the way I finally sat with mine.\\n\\nTry a pouch of Aurivita for 120 days. Track your morning erections from day one. Get a panel before you start and again at week ten. Let the data tell the story the blaming has been covering.\\n\\nIf morning erections have not returned by week three. If the panel has not moved at week ten. Return it. Every dollar back. No questions.\\n\\nMy wife asked me what changed. I told her I stopped blaming other things.\\n\\nShe said that was enough.\\n\\nIt is enough.\\n\\n👉 https://aurivita.co/collections/ed\\n\\n— Dr. Takeshi Sato\\nMale Sexual Medicine, 22 years\\n\\nP.S. Do not stop any medication without your physician's supervision. Bring the ingredient list to your next appointment. Ask about interactions. Then track your markers for 120 days and let the data make the argument the blaming has been preventing.\\n\\nP.P.S. Dry powder capsaicin fails on delivery. The liver clears it before it reaches the endothelium. Without oil-based delivery enabling lymphatic absorption the receptor never receives therapeutic concentration. Without piperine inhibiting clearance the capsaicin does not maintain plasma levels for sustained receptor occupancy. Aurivita is the formulation that normalised my Doppler and my panel. The three alternatives I tested failed on delivery.\\n\\nP.P.P.S. If you arrived here for the erection and stayed for the blood pressure and the cholesterol and the triglycerides and the energy — that is not incidental. It is one uncoupled enzyme expressing one failure across multiple vascular beds simultaneously. The enzyme has not been recoupled by any prescription in your current regimen. That is the gap. That is what TRPV1 activation addresses. That is why the markers move together when the enzyme finally flips back.\\n\\n👉 https://aurivita.co/collections/ed\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"LEARN MORE\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"http://try.aurivita.co/pdp\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"try.aurivita.co\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Declining erections predict heart attacks. Published data.\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":\"\\\"My doctor put me on a statin when my LDL hit 184 — and the muscle aches were unbearable. My legs hurt going up the stairs. I stopped the statin and started Aurivita instead. Ten weeks later my LDL was 138, my HDL went up, and I walked nine holes of golf without my legs hurting once.\\\"\"},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":13830,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":124,\"duplicates\":2,\"reachDelta1d\":462