formin does.\\n\\nMost capsaicin supplements are cheap ground bell pepper in dry capsules that die in your stomach. Aurivita Capsaicin Power uses pharmaceutical-grade capsaicin oil-suspended in cold-pressed avocado oil so it actually reaches your bloodstream. Stacked with Berberine for blood sugar support, Cinnamon for post-meal spikes, and BioPerine for absorption. Third-party tested for toxins and heavy metals. Now available on Amazon Prime.\\n\\nPatients are reporting:\\n🔸 Tingling reducing within the first week\\n🔸 Fatigue lifting by week three\\n🔸 Morning glucose dropping by week four\\n🔸 A1c moving by week eight — often for the first time in years\\n\\n🛡️ 120-day money-back guarantee.\\n🟠 50% off while the sale's on. Tap below.\",\"transcript\":\"If your metformin isn't stopping your neuropathy from getting worse, and nobody warned you, I'm a cardiologist and I will answer the 12 most common questions I get. Question one: When does diabetes actually become serious? A blood sugar reading that's a little high after a big meal? Probably nothing. But fasting glucose over 130 every single morning, tingling in your feet at night, and fatigue that sleep doesn't fix? That means your blood vessels have been damaged by high blood sugar long enough to cause real problems. And the longer the sugar stays elevated, the more the microscopic vessels in your feet, eyes, and organs are choking shut. Question two. I read the real problem isn't blood sugar itself. It's the damage to the vessels caused by the sugar. Is this true? Yes. And it's the part of the conversation most people never get to. Blood sugar was never really the enemy. Your body needs glucose, and at normal levels, it's the fuel that keeps every cell alive. It only becomes dangerous when years of elevated glucose have damaged the microscopic vessels feeding your feet, your kidneys, and every other organ. That damaged circulation is what causes the neuropathy, the fatigue, and the slow healing wounds. So when people cut carbs, drink more water, or take another supplement and watch their neuropathy get worse anyway, that is part of why. They are working on the sugar when the actual problem is whether the vessels have been damaged beyond simple sugar management. Question three, what symptoms should I look for? Diabetes itself is mostly silent until it isn't. Then suddenly you're waking up at 3AM exhausted. Your feet burn or tingle every night and you can't recover from meals anymore. But if you're already on Metformin and you notice constant nausea, exhaustion that no amount of sleep fixes, and your feet keep getting worse despite better numbers on paper, don't write that off as age. That is usually the drug and I'll get to why. Question four, my doctor says my A1C is borderline diabetic. Am I fine? Borderline means it is heading the wrong way. By the time it crosses into clearly diabetic, the vessels in your feet, eyes, and kidneys have already been damaged for years. The blood test is the last thing to catch up, not the first. Question five. I've been cutting carbs and taking cinnamon capsules. Why isn't it working? Because cutting carbs alone was never enough to reverse the damage. Your body damages your vessels every time glucose stays elevated too long, and years of that damage doesn't come back with a low carb diet. But even that misses the point because the problem was never how high your blood sugar is on a given day, it is how damaged the vessels are from years of elevated glucose. So when you skip breakfast or take another cinnamon capsule, you are working on the sugar side of the equation, while the damaged vessels that actually cause the neuropathy and fatigue stay untouched. Question six: What happens if I just leave it alone? It does not stay where it is. The vessels keep getting damaged and the neuropathy keeps spreading, and eventually the conversation in your doctor's office stops being about carb counting and starts being about insulin injections, amputation risk, kidney dialysis, or full diabetic complications you did not see coming. Question seven: Why isn't this taught more? Because endocrinology is trained to manage type two diabetes with metformin, not reverse the underlying cause. Metformin lowers the number on your meter. It does nothing about the vessel damage from years of high blood sugar. And that same drug that lowers your glucose also depletes your B12 and does nothing to protect the tiny vessels in your feet. Which is why so many patients on metformin feel constantly nauseated, watch their neuropathy get worse anyway, and get told it is just diabetic complications. Question eight: So what actually works? Research on capsaicin shows it does something no other compound does: because it reaches the microscopic vessels damaged by diabetes and reopens them. Capsaicin is the active compound in cayenne, but not the kind you're thinking of. Most diabetes supplements work like buckshot, scattering through the body and treating symptoms a little. Capsaicin works like a guided missile. It fires the switch that restarts your nitric oxide production, and reopens the vessels that have been half damaged for years. And because it never touches your blood sugar production the way metformin does, it doesn't cause the nausea, the B12 depletion, and the exhaustion. So you get your circulation back without the side effects. Question nine: How long until my symptoms move? It varies depending on where you are starting from, but here is what I usually see. By week one, most patients tell me the first thing they feel is the tingling in their feet reducing and energy coming back. That is the capsaicin getting your circulation flowing again well before the glucose numbers have started to come down. By week three, the fatigue starts to lift and morning glucose readings begin dropping. Around week four is when I start asking patients to come back in for blood work, because that is usually when the underlying changes are actually measurable. And by week eight, most patients I have followed are seeing a meaningful drop in their A1C and neuropathy symptoms, often for the first time in years. Some move faster than that, some take a little longer if they have been sitting at high blood sugar for over a decade. Question 10: Does this work for younger men, too? Vessel damage from elevated blood sugar starts in your 40s. If anything, men in their forties and fifties often see the fastest change because the vessel damage hasn't been sitting there for as long. Question 11: What brand should I take? Most capsaicin supplements are not doing much. They use cheap ground bell pepper with capsaicin levels below point 1% and then pack it in a dry capsule that dies in your stomach before it ever reaches bloodstream. You are paying for red dust. The one I usually recommend is Oriveda. They use pharmaceutical grade capsaicin oil suspended in cold pressed avocado oil, so it actually reaches your bloodstream, stacked with berberine for blood sugar support, cinnamon for post meal glucose spikes, and bioperine for absorption. They also third party test every batch for toxins and heavy metals. Question 12. How can I be sure it's going to work? Honestly, you can't be sure of anything before you try. That goes for metformin too. What I tell my patients is that this one comes with a hundred and twenty day money back guarantee, which is the main reason I am comfortable mentioning a specific brand at all. So if it doesn't work for you, you send it back and you get your money back. That is not nothing. Most of the things your doctor will hand you do not come with that. Anyway, I'll drop the link below if you wanna check it out. Those are the questions I answer every day. If you have any other questions about your diabetes, drop them in the comments and I will do my best to get to all of them.\",\"callToAction\":\"SHOP NOW\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://aurivita.co/products/capsaicin-power-diabetes\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"aurivita.co\",\"ctaDescription\":\"If Metformin isn't stopping the tingling in your feet, this is why.\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":null},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":19195,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":173,\"duplicates\":25,\"reachDelta1d\":3,\"reachDelta7d\":41,\"reachDelta30d\":362},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":[\"AU\",\"CA\",\"GB\",\"NZ\",\"SG\",\"US\"],\"mainCountry\":\"GB\",\"gender\":null,\"ageMin\":null,\"ageMax\":null},\"rank\":{\"positionInPage\":45,\"currentRank\":45,\"rankDelta\":-3,\"improvementPct\":14.38},\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":null,\"isLowReach\":true,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}}],\"pagination\":{\"page\":1,\"limit\":1,\"total\":136,\"totalPages\":136,\"offset\":0,\"isTotalApproximate\":false},\"meta\":{\"query\":null,\"keywords\":[\"cayenne\",\"neuropathy\"],\"searchIn\":\"ad_copy\",\"categoryIds\":[],\"country\":null,\"countryFilterKind\":\"audienceCountry\",\"adCountries\":null,\"mainCountries\":[],\"trendSignal\":\"reach_growth_7d\",\"sortBy\":\"reach\",\"order\":\"desc\",\"requestedLimit\":1,\"effectiveLimit\":1,\"activeOnly\":true,\"status\":\"active\",\"page\":1,\"minTrafficGrowthPercent\":null,\"trafficGrowthPeriod\":\"last30d\",\"trafficGrowth\":null,\"adsGrowth\":null,\"pageReachGrowth\":null,\"adReachGrowth\":null,\"language\":null,\"adLanguages\":[],\"cta\":[],\"currency\":null,\"currencies\":[],\"shopifyAppIds\":[],\"excludeShopifyAppIds\":[],\"pixelIds\":[],\"excludePixelIds\":[],\"themeIds\":[],\"filters\":{\"search\":[\"cayenne\",\"neuropathy\"],\"searchType\":\"adCopy\",\"keywordMode\":\"all\",\"sortBy\":\"reach\",\"order\":\"desc\",\"page\":1,\"limit\":1,\"platforms\":[\"facebook\"],\"status\":\"active\"},\"warnings\":[],\"paginationMaxOffset\":10000},\"fieldGuide\":{\"countryBreakdowns\":[{\"path\":\"data[].audience.targetedCountries\",\"meaning\":\"Full ad targeting country list, including non-EU markets such as US.\"},{\"path\":\"data[].audience.mainCountry\",\"meaning\":\"Primary audience country f