,\"failurePhase\":null,\"releaseAttempted\":false,\"releaseSucceeded\":null}}","_meta":{"io.modelcontextprotocol/serverInfo":{"name":"trendtrack-server","version":"0.0.1"}},"structuredContent":{"requestId":"23435a2f-24d1-4493-bda2-563a7c836238","data":[{"id":"facebook_1063042329874280","collationId":"1122250660277070","platform":"facebook","status":"active","createdAt":"2026-09-15T00:33:43.469Z","firstSeenAt":"2026-09-14T07:00:00.000Z","lastSeenAt":"2026-09-28T12:35:04.692Z","daysRunning":71,"media":{"type":"image","thumbnailUrl":null,"mediaUrl":"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/image/185dc2ed98f19712af65e312aecc850f4587ba0a5be35417ca46e0da8350a898.png"},"advertiser":{"id":"944867892040477","name":"Men's Health Insider","logoUrl":"https://medias.trendtrack.io/profile_picture/944867892040477.jpg","facebookPageId":"944867892040477","liveAdsCount":530,"reach30d":268368,"totalReach":3971285,"countriesCount":28,"facebookLikes":1604,"instagramFollowers":null},"content":{"title":null,"body":"The first thing a man with erectile dysfunction does is nothing.\n\nHe does not book an appointment. He does not tell his wife what is actually happening. He does not mention it to his physician. He waits. He finds a reason — the stress of the quarter, the late nights, the wine at dinner — and he holds onto that reason because the alternative requires a conversation he does not know how to start. And because the reason is always available, and always plausible, and always easier than the truth, the thing that is actually happening continues happening without anyone ever addressing it.\n\nI have been a specialist in sexual medicine for 21 years. I trained in Germany, where this field is taken more seriously than it is here, and where the conversation between a physician and a patient about sexual function is treated as the cardiovascular conversation it actually is rather than the embarrassing sidebar it tends to be in an American clinical setting. I came to the United States in 2009 and I have spent 17 years watching men redefine normal in ways that their counterparts in Germany would not have been permitted to do — because the German physician sitting across from them would have named what was happening, explained the mechanism, and refused to accept that declining sexual function and rising blood pressure and a cholesterol panel that keeps shifting are three separate problems managed by three separate departments.\n\nThey are not three separate problems. I want to explain what they actually are.\n\nBut first I want to tell you about the 14 months between my 51st birthday and the morning I finally ran my own workup and stopped redefining normal.\n\nMy name is Dr. Thomas Kessler. I am 53 years old. I specialize in male sexual medicine and vascular sexual health. I have treated erectile dysfunction as a primary vascular diagnosis — not a psychological condition, not a hormonal condition, but a vascular one — for my entire career. I have published on the relationship between endothelial function and erectile capacity. I have lectured on the cardiovascular significance of ED onset in men over 45. I have told more men than I can count that their declining erections are a blood vessel problem and not a confidence problem and not an age problem and not a stress problem.\n\nI had been redefining normal for 14 months before I gave that speech to myself.\n\nMy erections had become unreliable in 2023. I attributed this — with the specific, practiced fluency of a man who knows exactly what language to deploy against himself — to the transition of my practice, to the hours, to a difficult year personally, to being 51. I had all of the clinical knowledge and none of the clinical honesty that I would have applied immediately to a patient presenting with the same history.\n\nMy wife said nothing. She is German, like me, which means she is direct about most things but understands that there are certain conversations a person must arrive at on their own schedule. She waited. She gave me 14 months. And then one evening she said, with the particular precision of someone who has chosen their words carefully over a long period of time, that she felt we had been living alongside each other rather than with each other, and that she wanted to understand why.\n\nI went to my office the next morning and ran the workup I had been not running on myself for 14 months.\n\nThe Doppler was unambiguous. Reduced peak systolic velocity in the bilateral cavernosal arteries. Impaired hemodynamic response to pharmacostimulation. The arterial inflow pattern of a 51-year-old man whose endothelium had been under sustained inflammatory assault for years.\n\nI looked at the printout for a long time.\n\nThen I looked at my last cardiometabolic panel. LDL 158, up from 129 three years earlier. Triglycerides 204, up from 168. Blood pressure averaging 141 over 89, up from 124 over 81. Fasting glucose 103. Trending.\n\nFour markers. All moving in the same direction over the same period. Alongside the erectile function decline I had been redefining as normal.\n\nI have been lecturing on this correlation for 17 years. I had not applied it to my own data for 14 months because I was too busy redefining normal.\n\nLet me explain what all of these markers share, because it is the explanation I should have been starting every patient consultation with for two decades and have instead been burying at the end after the prescription has already been written.\n\nThe innermost lining of every blood vessel in your body is a single continuous sheet of cells called the endothelium. When these cells function correctly, they express an enzyme — eNOS, endothelial nitric oxide synthase — that produces nitric oxide continuously. Nitric oxide is the molecule that tells the surrounding smooth muscle of the vessel wall to relax. When nitric oxide production is adequate, vessels stay open without external override. Blood pressure is controlled from within the wall. LDL particles passing through the arterial space do not adhere to the wall surface and build plaque. And when sexual arousal occurs, the rapid high-volume arterial inflow that fills the corpora cavernosa and produces an erection proceeds exactly as it was designed to.\n\nWhen the endothelium is damaged — by the chronic low-grade inflammation that accumulates across decades of metabolic stress, by elevated triglycerides oxidizing the vessel wall, by the sustained hemodynamic pressure of blood pressure that has been creeping without being addressed at the source — eNOS expression falls. The vessels lose autonomous tone regulation. Blood pressure rises. LDL catches against an inflamed wall and builds. And the helicine arteries — the finest diameter vessels in the male body, the ones feeding the corpus cavernosum — constrict and lose the capacity for the rapid inflow an erection requires.\n\nThe erectile vessels fail first not because something is specifically wrong there but because vessel failure presents first where vessel diameter is smallest. The same endothelial dysfunction that is producing the unreliable erections is producing the rising blood pressure and the LDL that keeps climbing and the triglycerides that your internist watches and calls a trend. These are not separate conditions. They are one endothelium expressing one failure across four readouts simultaneously.\n\nThe Viagra your GP prescribed amplifies whatever nitric oxide signal the endothelium is still producing. When eNOS function is relatively preserved, the amplification is clinically useful. As eNOS expression continues to fall with progressive endothelial deterioration, the signal being amplified diminishes. This is the pharmacological explanation for why the PDE5 inhibitor that worked at 51 works less reliably at 55 and unreliably at 58 — not because the drug has changed but because the eNOS signal it depends on has continued to fall. Amplifying a disappearing signal does not slow its disappearance. It